Crystal 1.22.0 for wasm32-wasi: exceptions, struct returns, Boehm GC, WASI file system fixes, optimized builds,
layout numbers.

A derivative of Crystal (https://github.com/crystal-lang/crystal, Apache License 2.0). Applies to tag 1.22.0
(commit 39f3f4d08f70c3678b579f85dc6f8b7be69c7956) with `git apply`; tools/wasm/setup.sh does that.

What it changes, and why:
- codegen/exception.cr, codegen/codegen.cr, raise.cr, compiler.cr, llvm/: wasm32 takes the funclet exception path
  MSVC takes (one catch-all catchpad; llvm.wasm.get.exception yields the thrown Crystal Exception, raise is
  llvm.wasm.throw) with LLVM's -wasm-enable-eh and +exception-handling. Stock 1.22: `raise` on wasm32 exits(1).
  __crystal_raise is @[NoInline]: inlined into a rescue body, its call of llvm.wasm.throw would become an invoke,
  which LLVM forbids (at -O1 and above the compiler died in instruction selection) - as __cxa_throw keeps it in C++.
- compiler.cr, llvm/lib_llvm/core.cr: after the optimizer (wasm32 only), a direct call or invoke whose function type
  differs from its callee's with an aggregate parameter or return on either side calls an internal stub of its
  own type whose body is `unreachable` instead. The optimizer makes such calls out of the branch of a proc call
  that never runs for the proc at hand (closure data on a plain function, or none on a closure's); LLVM's
  WebAssembly backend adapts mismatched calls without aggregates (WebAssemblyFixFunctionBitcasts - this stub is
  the one it gives calls it cannot adapt) but emits invalid wasm with one. A call with fewer arguments reads a
  missing one natively too; one with more is harmless natively and traps here - neither runs for a proc Crystal
  builds. CRYMBLE_WASM_STUB_REPORT=1 prints how many calls and invokes were retargeted.
- codegen/abi/wasm32.cr: a C function returning a struct returns it through a hidden pointer (StructRet), as
  clang does. Stock 1.22: ByVal there, so the call site never allocates the slot - the compiler crashes on any
  C function that returns a struct.
- gc.cr: wasm32 uses Boehm (built for WASI by setup.sh) like every other target. Stock 1.22: gc/none - nothing
  is ever freed. gc/boehm.cr: GC.init registers the static data (class variables, constants) as a root range
  through the toolchain's shim - Boehm's wasm config scans none, so a constant like STDOUT was collected and its
  finalizer closed the descriptor; a finalizer callback returns void whatever `finalize` returns (wasm traps on
  the mismatched signature); GC_set_stackbottom returns void in bdwgc >= 8.2 (the binding's return type
  made a signature-mismatch warning at link time).
- crystal/event_loop/wasi.cr: EventLoop#open via LibC.open (stock: NotImplementedError - no file could be opened).
- crystal/system/wasi/file.cr: File.delete uses the unix implementation (stock: NotImplementedError).
- crystal/system/wasi/dir.cr: a refill that comes back empty ends the listing (stock: an empty directory on a
  runtime without "." and ".." listed one entry with an empty name - FileUtils.rm_r recursed forever).
- codegen/codegen.cr, codegen/llvm_typer.cr, semantic/main_visitor.cr, semantic/cleanup_transformer.cr, compiler.cr:
  sizeof, alignof, instance_sizeof, instance_alignof and offsetof are folded once every type is complete - by the
  cleanup transformer - and while typing only inside a type argument (which needs the number then). Stock 1.22 folds
  them while typing through the program's LLVMTyper, which caches each layout: a field typed by a generic module
  whose including struct does not exist yet is laid out as a pointer, so `dup` (instance_sizeof) copies too few
  bytes and sizeof is too small - for later folds too. Any target: the stock compiler prints 0 for
  `Cell.new(Impl(Int32).new(1, 2), 42)` duplicated by a proc typed first, Impl(T) a struct of two Int64 including
  Iface(T), Cell's field typed Iface(Int32). Folds go through a fresh typer with its own LLVM context AND its own
  data layout: the target machine's is shared and caches struct layouts by address, so a freed context's struct
  would hand its layout to one codegen creates later at that address (objects allocated too small). Every fold is
  checked against the final layout before and after codegen; a difference is a compile error naming the expression.
- spec/expectations.cr: expect_raises works on wasm32 (the stub that skipped it is gone).

